f 17592186045417] [17592186161286 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5a574bd7-55e7-5497-8509-e1ea7e6267ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161287 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161287 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161287 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6a4d4628-0329-5af8-9fad-8ada59e15117" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161805 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8291" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161805 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ must have all real eigenvalues\n\n- $A$ is diagonalizable\n\n- If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are two eigenvectors, then $\\mathbf{u} \\perp \\mathbf{v}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161805 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ae71f41-133f-50b6-a286-20ae7e2c3a9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161805 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n \\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable. Hence, it is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues must be orthogonal, but two eigenvectors corresponding to the *same* eigenvalue may not be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161806 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161806 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ of the matrix ![](images/55/554407e7ec109f5d6e767d87900321d07c253cceefd6e1cdd21ec11351c4548e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161806 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d185ef42-c177-5217-99b7-b42d800f3385" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161806 #edb/ref 1074 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1\\cdot \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2\\cdot \\mathbf u_2^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, $\\mathbf u_i\\cdot \\mathbf u_i^T$ is the projection matrix corresponding to $\\mathbf u_i.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac12x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/3e/3e365c1786b35c426487c5555bccdb10c67545e5757c8e09b34c514d41ce9219.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/e9/e97e23e38548021d0a72c50ff7e34ed108070c482249b23f9cb54a6e812ecc77.png)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n![](images/23/23384122e613a5efe2858604b08a1d7353e1b6e08dbc0de4b958a4881a3bda1e.png)\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161807 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8293" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161807 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/af/af52b1cb9f0d922e4f9e08ebdf9ec4e268c163fcb536b91906b9982d1e188be1.png) and ![](images/0f/0fb0224f57e0a8d781e172af6dce6c7a6ca43b297a9de6930d47dfd7bf23cdc0.png)\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=6$ and $\\lambda_2=-7,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161807 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65b23c21-528f-5df7-a00c-8e2dce041276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161807 #edb/ref 1074 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1\\cdot \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2\\cdot \\mathbf u_2^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-7)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-7)\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-7)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]+(-7)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-39 & 78 \\\\ 78 & 26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162013 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162013 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162013 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162013 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162013 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162013 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162013 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162013 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162013 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162013 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162013 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162013 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162014 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162014 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162646 #edb/ref