tion of a Symmetric Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162015 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162015 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162216 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162216 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162216 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162216 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162216 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162216 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162216 #edb/ref 1045 "The Spectral Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162216 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162216 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162216 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "22c8da45-56d1-5645-85df-aa94cccb29d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162645 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162645 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162645 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A$ is a $5 \\cdot 5$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ must have $5$ distinct eigenvalues\n2. $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable\n3. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to the same eigenvalue, then $u⊥v$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162645 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15b6a411-2b41-5f8b-b306-30b41e9bfcc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162659 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162659 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95411" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162659 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ must have all real eigenvalues\n2. $A$ is diagonalizable\n3. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u = αv$ for some scalar $α$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162659 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbaacbd1-c58a-5f9b-94e2-0510581c4052" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162665 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162665 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162665 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95730" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162665 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162665 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162665 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "205194a3-9e3c-52b4-993f-f1572b1527e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162665 #edb/ref 1074 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 20 \\\\ 20 & -15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162849 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162849 #edb/ref 1061 30533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162849 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "84b2f14e-f6c7-5b6c-b6d7-19d4d7d71f71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162849 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162902 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162902 #edb/ref 1061 30970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162902 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "95f72e96-f84b-5b21-a070-253638be57a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162902 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162903 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162903 #edb/ref 1061 30971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162903 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8df507ec-4d53-59f1-b941-07907cd14b6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162903 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162904 #edb/ref 1061 30972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162904 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a1d5d86-333c-5361-87ec-fd7766797988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162904 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162905 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162905 #edb/ref 1061 30973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162905 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "86248bdd-6a75-54cd-8196-8b333a64cac5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162905 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163224 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}.$ The **spectral theorem** states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n- The eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163224 #edb/ref 1065 4882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163224 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5ecbbdd0-25a0-52ee-8f0c-987dcd8ba584" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163224 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163243 #edb/ref 1064 "We can write an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix $A$ in terms of a sum of products of its eigenvalues and eigenvectors.\n\nSpecifically, if $\\mathbf{u}_i$ denotes an orthonormal eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_i$ for $i=1,\\ldots, n$, then\n\n$$A=\\sum_{i=1}^n \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T.$$\n\nExpressing a symmetric matrix $A$ in this way is called the **spectral decomposition** of $A.$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the spectral decomposition of the $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$A = [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1 = 6$ and $\\lambda_2 = -4,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n$$\\mathbf{v}_1 = [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{v}_2 = [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nThe spectral decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n$$A =\\sum_{i=1}^2 \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1 \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2 \\mathbf u_2^T,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the spectral decomposition of $A$ is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\ & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & ={{6}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} & \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{9}{10}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}}+{{(-4)}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & -\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIn this decomposition, the matrix $\\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T$ is the orthogonal projection matrix that maps any $\\mathbf x \\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by $\\mathbf u_i.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163243 #edb/ref 1065 4973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163243 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a90957e3-e4b9-548d-930b-e060c6b63859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163243 #edb/ref 1067 "The Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365059 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365059 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140848" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365059 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365059 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365059 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3897ca8e-c190-5166-8001-b5cc763dcea3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365059 #edb/ref 1074 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365060 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365060 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365060 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365060 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365060 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365060 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365060 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365060 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186365061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365060 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1feaaa2b-9f91-5604-bad3-f9a41903fec2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365061 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365061 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365061 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "259bc7f0-def1-5dbe-84fa-df884b75de5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365062 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365062 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365062 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4ede6774-72a7-5e72-98ce-278b61c0729e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365063 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365063 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365063 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a6077b11-1139-5964-95d7-ab7c61b27c5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365064 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365064 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365064 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "55529e55-64a4-5791-b0a3-817ff749c29b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365065 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365065 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365065 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "941d3dcd-c846-51d5-8b37-ac5967b27e23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366643 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452949 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366643 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452950 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366643 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452951 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366643 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95409" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366643 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366643 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is not diagonalizable\n- **II.** If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$\n- **III.** $A$ must have $4$ real (not necessarily distinct) eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366643 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42059a97-8b39-59cb-bae6-eab5799ef586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366643 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the theorem, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, hence, is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, a $4\\times 4$ matrix has $4$ (not necessarily distinct) eigenvalues and the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366657 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452955 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366657 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452956 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366657 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452957 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366657 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95702" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366657 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366657 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ real symmetric matrix whose roots of the characteristic polynomial have multiplicity equal to $1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to the same eigenvalue, then $u$ is parallel to $v$\n- **II.** $A$ must have $7$ real and distinct eigenvalues\n- **III.** $A$ is orthogonal" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366657 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "531e02eb-ae49-5805-b616-b28b7ddc3a97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366657 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. But two eigenvectors that correspond to the same eigenvalue will always be parallel if the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue is $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real. Also, since the roots of the characteristic polynomial have (algebraic) multiplicity all equal to $1,$ the eigenvalues are all distinct.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, but not necessarily orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366670 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366670 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95705" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366670 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366670 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366670 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04f2e807-374a-54f0-9c1c-c36bd677ab53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366670 #edb/ref 1074 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366671 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366671 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/r